==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ML.}} {{term|actuatus|āctuātus|lang=la}}, perfect passive participle of {{term|actuo|āctuō|actuate, implement|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|actus|āctus|lang=la}}, perfect passive participle of {{term|ago|agō|do, act|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈæktʃu.eɪt/|/ˈæktju.eɪt/}}, {{SAMPA|/"{ktSu.eIt/|/"{ktju.eIt/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈæktʃu.eɪt/}}, {{SAMPA|/"{ktSu.eIt/}}
* {{audio|en-us-actuate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|actuat|ing}}

# {{transitive}} To [[activate]], or to put into [[motion]]; to [[animate]].
# {{transitive}} To [[incite]] to [[action]]; to [[motivate]].
#* '''1748'''.  HUME, David ''Enquiries concerning the human understanding and concerning the principles of moral.''  2. ed.  London: Oxford University Press, 1973.  § 11.
#*: A man in a fit of anger, is '''actuated''' in a very different manner from one who only thinks of that emotion.

